.TH "CSSCONTAINERRULE" "3JS" "June 6, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSContainerRule \- CSSContainerRule
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]CSSContainerRule\f[B]\f[R] interface represents a single
CSS \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule.
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
\f[I]Inherits properties from its ancestors \f[CI]CSSConditionRule\f[I],
\f[CI]CSSGroupingRule\f[I], and \f[CI]CSSRule\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]CSSContainerRule.conditions\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns an array of objects, each of which specifies a container
condition in a \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule.
The objects have a \f[CR]name\f[R] string property and a
\f[CR]query\f[R] string property, either of which may be the empty
string if not defined.
The \f[CR]name\f[R] represents the name of a container, and the
\f[CR]query\f[R] represents the set of feature tests that must be true
for the particular condition to apply.
.TP
\f[B]CSSContainerRule.containerName\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns a string representing the name of the container condition of a
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R], when there is just one condition.
If there are multiple container conditions, or if there is just one
condition that does not specify a name, this is the empty string.
.TP
\f[B]CSSContainerRule.containerQuery\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns a string representing the container query for the container
condition of a \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R], when there is only one
condition.
This represents a set of feature tests that must all be true for the
condition to apply.
If there are multiple container conditions, or if there is just one
condition that does not specify a query, this is the empty string.
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
\f[I]No specific methods; inherits methods from its ancestors
\f[CI]CSSConditionRule\f[I], \f[CI]CSSGroupingRule\f[I], and
\f[CI]CSSRule\f[I].\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] object represents a
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule.
.PP
A \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule defines one or more comma\-separated
\f[I]container conditions\f[R].
Each container condition consists of a \(lqname\(rq and/or a
\(lqquery\(rq, where the \(lqname\(rq indicates the name of the
container to which the condition applies and the \(lqquery\(rq specifies
one or more logically combined feature checks on a container\(cqs
properties.
If any of the container conditions match a container, the indicated
styles are applied.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Support for multiple container conditions is indicated
by the \f[CR]conditions\f[R] key in the Browser compatibility table
(earlier versions of the specification allowed only a single container
condition).
This affects how \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] and
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] are used.
.RE
.PP
A contrived example consisting of three conditions is shown below.
This will match a container named \f[CR]main\-content\f[R] if its width
is between \f[CR]600px\f[R] and \f[CR]800px\f[R], any container that has
a height greater than \f[CR]800px\f[R], or any container named
\f[CR]other\-content\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] main\-content (\f[B]width\f[R] > 600px) \f[B]and\f[R] (\f[B]width\f[R] < 800px), (\f[B]height\f[R] > 800px), other\-content {
  \f[I]/* Apply styles */\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
In supporting browsers, the \f[CR]CSSContainerRule.conditions\f[R]
property represents a \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] as an array of objects,
each of which defines a single container condition.
The objects have the properties \f[CR]name\f[R] and \f[CR]query\f[R],
which may be the empty string (\f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]).
The \f[CR]conditions\f[R] property for the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R]
example above would look like this:
.IP
.EX
[
  { name: \(dqmain\-content\(dq, query: \(dq(width > 600px) and (width < 800px)\(dq },
  { name: \(dq\(dq, query: \(dq(height > 800px)\(dq },
  { name: \(dqother\-content\(dq, query: \(dq\(dq },
];
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]containerName\f[R] and \f[CR]containerQuery\f[R] properties
predate support for container rules with multiple container conditions.
For a container rule with a \f[I]single container condition\f[R] they
contain the name and query of that condition (mirroring the
\f[CR]name\f[R] and \f[CR]query\f[R] properties of the object in the
\f[CR]conditions\f[R] array).
For a container rule with multiple conditions they are both set to the
empty string.
.PP
Note that browsers that don\(cqt support the \f[CR]conditions\f[R]
property only allow container rules with a single container condition.
An \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] with multiple container conditions will not
be parsed, and no corresponding \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] will be
created.
.PP
You can also get the text for the whole condition using
\f[CR]CSSConditionRule.conditionText\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Feature testing
Feature testing can be complicated because you may need to handle cases
where \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] or
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule.conditions\f[R] are not supported, and also the
particular case where \f[CR]conditions\f[R] is not supported but
multiple container conditions have been specified in the CSS.
.PP
This code shows how you can do it, assuming that you have already
obtained \f[CR]containerRule\f[R], a \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R]
instance that corresponds to an \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule defined in
the page CSS (the next example shows how you might get
\f[CR]containerRule\f[R]).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (\f[B]typeof\f[R] CSSContainerRule === \(dqundefined\(dq) {
  \f[I]// Browser doesn\(aqt support CSSContainerRule (at all)\f[R]
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule is not supported in this browser.\(dq);
} \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (!containerRule) {
  \f[I]// Browser doesn\(aqt support multiple container conditions\f[R]
  log(
    \(dqNo CSSContainerRule was created \(em \(atcontainer with multiple conditions may not be parsed.\(dq,
  );
} \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (\(dqconditions\(dq \f[B]in\f[R] CSSContainerRule.prototype) {
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions is supported.\(dq);
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions:\(dq);
  containerRule.conditions.forEach((item) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] jsonString = JSON.stringify(item);
    log(\(ga  ${jsonString}\(ga);
  });
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.conditionText: \(dq${containerRule.conditionText}\(dq\(ga);
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  \f[I]// \(atcontainer exists but predates the multi\-condition specification\f[R]
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions not supported\(dq);
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.containerName: \(dq${containerRule.containerName}\(dq\(ga);
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.containerQuery: \(dq${containerRule.containerQuery}\(dq\(ga);
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.conditionText: \(dq${containerRule.conditionText}\(dq\(ga);
}
.EE
.PP
Note that if it is defined, we prefer to use the information in
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule.conditions\f[R] rather than
\f[CR]containerName\f[R] and \f[CR]containerQuery\f[R].
.SS Unnamed container condition
The example below defines a \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule that has a
single container condition with no name, and displays the properties of
the associated \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R].
The CSS is the same as in the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] example Setting
styles based on a container\(cqs size.
.PP
Note that our code to log the results is not particularly relevant, so
it has been hidden.
.SS HTML
First, we define the HTML for a \f[CR]card\f[R] contained within a
\f[CR]post\f[R].
These are represented by two nested \f[CR]<div>\f[R] elements.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqpost\(dq>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqcard\(dq>
    <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Card title</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
    <\f[B]p\f[R]>Card content</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  </\f[B]div\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
The CSS for the example is shown below.
The CSS first specifies the \f[CR]container\-type\f[R] for the container
element (\f[CR]post\f[R]).
The \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule then applies a new \f[CR]width\f[R],
\f[CR]background\-color\f[R], and \f[CR]font\-size\f[R] to the card if
the width is less than \f[CR]650px\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]style\f[R] id=\(dqexample\-styles\(dq>
  \f[I]/* A container context based on inline size */\f[R]
  .post {
    \f[B]container\-type\f[R]: inline\-size;
  }

  \f[I]/* Apply styles if the container is narrower than 650px */\f[R]
  \f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] (\f[B]width\f[R] < 650px) {
    .card {
      \f[B]width\f[R]: 50%;
      \f[B]background\-color\f[R]: gray;
      \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 1em;
    }
  }
</\f[B]style\f[R]>
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] The styles in these examples are defined in an inline
HTML \f[CR]<style>\f[R] element with an \f[CR]id\f[R], to make it easy
for the code to find the correct style sheet.
You might also locate the correct style sheet for each example by
indexing against the number of style sheets contained in the document,
that is, the \f[CR]length\f[R] of the \f[CR]styleSheets\f[R] property
(for example,
\f[CR]document.styleSheets[document.styleSheets.length\-1]\f[R]), but
that makes working out the correct sheet for each example more
complicated.
.RE
.SS JavaScript
The code below gets the \f[CR]HTMLStyleElement\f[R] associated with the
example using its \f[CR]id\f[R], and then uses its \f[CR]sheet\f[R]
property to get the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R].
From the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R] we get the set of \f[CR]cssRules\f[R]
added to the sheet.
Since we added the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] as the second rule above, we
can access the associated \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] using the second
entry, with index \(lq1\(rq in the \f[CR]cssRules\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleStylesheet = document.getElementById(\(dqexample\-styles\(dq).sheet;
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleRules = exampleStylesheet.cssRules;
\f[B]const\f[R] containerRule = exampleRules[1]; \f[I]// a CSSContainerRule representing the container rule.\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Next, we use our feature testing code from the previous example to find
and log the information we want to display.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (\f[B]typeof\f[R] CSSContainerRule === \(dqundefined\(dq) {
  \f[I]// Browser doesn\(aqt support CSSContainerRule (at all)\f[R]
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule is not supported in this browser.\(dq);
} \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (!containerRule) {
  \f[I]// Browser doesn\(aqt support multiple container conditions\f[R]
  log(
    \(dqNo CSSContainerRule was created. This browser doesn\(aqt support \(atcontainer with multiple conditions.\(dq,
  );
} \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (\(dqconditions\(dq \f[B]in\f[R] CSSContainerRule.prototype) {
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions is supported.\(dq);
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions:\(dq);
  containerRule.conditions.forEach((item) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] jsonString = JSON.stringify(item);
    log(\(ga  ${jsonString}\(ga);
  });
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.conditionText: \(dq${containerRule.conditionText}\(dq\(ga);
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  \f[I]// \(atcontainer exists but predates the multi\-condition specification\f[R]
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions not supported\(dq);
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.containerName: \(dq${containerRule.containerName}\(dq\(ga);
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.containerQuery: \(dq${containerRule.containerQuery}\(dq\(ga);
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.conditionText: \(dq${containerRule.conditionText}\(dq\(ga);
}
.EE
.SS Results
The example output is shown below.
This lists the condition using either the \f[CR]conditions\f[R] property
if it is supported or \f[CR]containerName\f[R]/\f[CR]containerQuery\f[R]
if it is not.
.PP
Note that the card\(cqs \f[CR]background\-color\f[R] should change when
the container width becomes smaller or larger than \f[CR]650px\f[R].
.SS Named container condition
The example below defines a \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule that includes
a name and a query, and displays the properties of the associated
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R].
.PP
The CSS is very similar to that in the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] example
Creating named container contexts.
Note that we\(cqve hidden the HTML, logging code, and feature checking
code, as they are the same as in the previous example.
.SS CSS
In this example, the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] specifies a container
name, \f[CR]sidebar\f[R], as well as the container type.
The card has a default font size, which is overridden when it is
contained inside a \f[CR]sidebar\f[R] \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] when its
width is greater than or equal to \f[CR]700px\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]style\f[R] id=\(dqexample\-styles\(dq>
  .post {
    \f[B]container\-type\f[R]: inline\-size;
    \f[B]container\-name\f[R]: sidebar;
  }

  \f[I]/* Default heading styles for the card title */\f[R]
  .card h2 {
    \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 1em;
  }

  \f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] sidebar (\f[B]width\f[R] >= 700px) {
    .card {
      \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 2em;
    }
  }
</\f[B]style\f[R]>
.EE
.SS Results
The example output is shown below.
This lists the condition using either the \f[CR]conditions\f[R] property
if it is supported or \f[CR]containerName\f[R]/\f[CR]containerQuery\f[R]
if it is not.
The \f[CR]conditionText\f[R] is also logged, and shows the combination
of these two strings.
.PP
The text in the card \f[CR]<div>\f[R] should double in size as the page
width reaches \f[CR]700px\f[R], and halve as it goes below
\f[CR]700px\f[R] again.
.SS Multiple container conditions
The example below defines a \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule that includes
multiple container conditions, and displays the properties of the
associated \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R].
.PP
Note that we\(cqve hidden the HTML, logging code, and feature checking
code, as they are the same as in the previous example.
.SS CSS
The \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] declaration here defines two container
conditions; it will match a container if either condition is true.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]style\f[R] id=\(dqexample\-styles\(dq>
  .post {
    \f[B]container\-type\f[R]: inline\-size;
    \f[B]container\-name\f[R]: sidebar;
  }

  \f[I]/* Default heading styles for the card title */\f[R]
  .card h2 {
    \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 1em;
  }

  \f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] sidebar (\f[B]width\f[R] <= 600px), (\f[B]aspect\-ratio\f[R] > 1/1) {
    .card {
      \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 2em;
      \f[B]background\-color\f[R]: lightblue;
    }
  }
</\f[B]style\f[R]>
.EE
.SS Results
The example output is shown below.
Browsers that support the \f[CR]conditions\f[R] property will show both
conditions.
Those that do not will log a note indicating that multiple conditions
cannot be parsed.
